```

Note: Replace each "XXX" with the variable you’re testing. Make sure your program executes in debug mode, before running it.--------------------To obtain the assert statements in the provided C# code, you need to use regular expressions (regex) to match all lines that start with "@@@". However, it's important to note that the provided code does not include any assert statements in lines starting with "@@@". 

However, suppose you had the following code:
```cs
using Microsoft.Pex.Framework;

public class MyClass {
    // @@@ This is a comment
    public void MyMethod(int x) {
        int oldValue = x;
        x += 1;
        // @@@ This is also a comment
        int newValue = x;
    }
} 
```
You can use the following Python code:

```python
import re

code_text = """
using Microsoft.Pex.Framework;

public class MyClass {
    // @@@ This is a comment
    public void MyMethod(int x) {
        int oldValue = x;
        x += 1;
        // @@@ This is also a comment
        int newValue = x;
    }
} 
"""

# The regex pattern for matching lines starting with "@@@"
pattern = r"^\s*@@@"

matches = re.findall(pattern, code_text, re.MULTILINE)

for match in matches:
    print(f"Debug.Assert({match});")
```

This returns all lines starting with "@@@", removing the "@@@" part:

```
Debug.Assert( This is a comment);
Debug.Assert( This is also a comment);
```

